    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6927A\n\nSPONSOR: ADDABBO\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to establish a commission to be known as the\n\"New York seawall study commission\"; and providing for the repeal of\nsuch provisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo set up a commission to study the feasibility, costs, impacts, and\nlocations of a seawall around the city of New York's coastline.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSets up a 13 member commission (appointed for the life of the commis-\nsion) as follows\n\n-3 members appointed by the temporary president of the Senate\n-1 member appointed by the minority leader of the Senate\n-3 members appointed by the speaker of the Assembly\n-1 member appointed by the minority leader of the Assembly\n-5 members appointed by the Governor\n\nThe commission will hold hearings across the state and conduct a compre-\nhensive study on sea levels and a potential seawall on the New York City\ncoastline. The matters studied by the commission shall include, but not\nbe limited to:\n\n-Coastal areas at greatest risk from sea level rise;the future -Areas at\nrisk from storm surge with current sea levels;being created surge along\nthe\n-Areas that are likely to be at risk from coastal hazards in through\nyear 2100; and\n-The feasibility, costs, impacts, and locations of a seawall to protect\nthe city of New York from sea level rise and storm entire coastline\n\nAmended Provisions:\n\nChanges the commission from a 13 to a 14 member commission\n\n-Commissioner of environmental conservation or his/her designee\n-Secretary of State or his/her designee\n-2 members appointed by the Governor\n-2 members appointed by the temporary president of the senate\n-2 members appointed by the Speaker of the Assembly\n-1 member appointed by the leader of the Senate\n-1 member appointed by the minority leader of the Assembly\n-2 members to be appointed by the Mayor of NYC\n-1 member appointed by the County Executive of Nassau\n-1 member appointed by the County Executive of Suffolk\n\nAllows recommendations on state and local regulatory and/or statutory\nalterations.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn just the last few years, we have seen one horrific storm after anoth-\ner. These once in a lifetime storms are becoming more and more common.\nNew York City must be prepared for the next Hurricane Sandy.  A seawall\nis one idea that could possibly save lives in the next super-storm, as\nwell as protect vital infrastructure that could cost billions to repair.\nThis piece of legislation would create a commission to study the New\nYork City coastline to determine the feasibility of a seawall. In 1900,\na Category 4 hurricane, the \"Great Storm\", struck Galveston, Texas kill-\ning 6,000 to 12,000 individuals.  It was the deadliest natural disaster\nin U.S history. In 1915, a similar storm in strength struck Galveston,\nbut the seawall proved invaluable for protection of the city and its\nresidents. A seawall as seen in Galveston, can help save lives and\nprotect infrastructure. A study of how such a seawall can be of vital\nservice to the New York City coast makes perfect sense.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
